    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is the primary advantage of a surgical cricothyrotomy over a formal tracheostomy in an emergency "Cannot Intubate, Cannot Ventilate" (CICV) scenario?', '["It provides a larger diameter airway", "It avoids the highly vascular thyroid gland and is faster to perform", "It has a lower rate of long-term subglottic stenosis", "It is easier to perform in pediatric patients"]', 1, 'Cricothyrotomy requires a superficial incision through the relatively avascular cricothyroid membrane, making it significantly faster and safer in emergencies than dissecting through the neck to the trachea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'To perform a surgical cricothyrotomy, the clinician must palpate the cricothyroid membrane, which is located between the:', '["Hyoid bone and thyroid cartilage", "Thyroid cartilage (superiorly) and cricoid cartilage (inferiorly)", "Cricoid cartilage (superiorly) and first tracheal ring (inferiorly)", "Sternal notch and thyroid gland"]', 1, 'The cricothyroid membrane connects the thyroid cartilage above to the cricoid cartilage below.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a surgical cricothyrotomy, what is the recommended direction for the initial skin incision and the subsequent membrane incision?', '["Horizontal skin incision, vertical membrane incision", "Vertical skin incision, horizontal membrane incision", "Vertical skin incision, vertical membrane incision", "Horizontal skin incision, horizontal membrane incision"]', 1, 'A vertical skin incision avoids the anterior jugular veins laterally and allows for extension if landmarks are lost. The stab incision through the membrane itself should be horizontal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the modern "scalpel-finger-bougie" technique for cricothyrotomy, the primary purpose of inserting the finger into the incision is to:', '["Dilate the membrane hole", "Palpate the vocal cords", "Maintain the tract, bluntly dissect tissue, and guide the bougie", "Prevent bleeding from the anterior jugular veins"]', 2, 'The finger maintains the tract through the skin and membrane, confirming entry into the airway and allowing the bougie to slide smoothly over the finger into the trachea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Surgical cricothyrotomy is absolutely contraindicated in which patient population?', '["Morbidly obese adults", "Patients with severe facial trauma", "Children under the age of 8 to 10 years", "Patients with massive hematemesis"]', 2, 'In young children, the larynx is small and funnel-shaped, and the cricoid cartilage is the narrowest part of the airway. Surgical cricothyrotomy risks destroying the cricoid, leading to permanent structural damage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The preferred rescue airway technique for a 4-year-old child in a CICV scenario is:', '["Surgical cricothyrotomy", "Retrograde intubation", "Percutaneous needle cricothyrotomy with translaryngeal jet ventilation", "Blind nasotracheal intubation"]', 2, 'Needle cricothyrotomy (usually 12-14 gauge) with jet ventilation is the safest temporizing measure for children under 8-10 years old.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Translaryngeal jet ventilation (via a needle cricothyrotomy) requires a high-pressure oxygen source. What is the standard required pressure?', '["15 psi", "50 psi", "100 psi", "Wall flow meter set at 15 L/min"]', 1, 'Jet ventilation requires a high-pressure source, typically 50 psi directly from a wall outlet or oxygen cylinder, to drive adequate volume through a small-bore catheter.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When performing translaryngeal jet ventilation, what is the most critical physiological requirement to avoid severe barotrauma (e.g., tension pneumothorax)?', '["Complete upper airway obstruction", "An I:E (Inspiratory to Expiratory) ratio of 1:4 to allow passive exhalation through the upper airway", "Simultaneous bag-valve-mask ventilation", "Continuous suctioning of the oropharynx"]', 1, 'Jet ventilation is completely active for inspiration but relies on passive exhalation through the native upper airway. If exhalation is not allowed (e.g., complete upper airway obstruction), air trapping and barotrauma rapidly occur.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is the most common serious complication of a PERCUTANEOUS needle cricothyrotomy?', '["Vocal cord paralysis", "Esophageal perforation", "Subcutaneous emphysema and barotrauma", "Massive hemorrhage"]', 2, 'Because it utilizes high-pressure ventilation, catheter dislodgement or inadequate exhalation commonly leads to massive subcutaneous emphysema or pneumothorax.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common error/complication associated with SURGICAL cricothyrotomy?', '["Pneumothorax", "Placement of the tube into a false tract (subcutaneous tissue)", "Laceration of the carotid artery", "Complete transection of the trachea"]', 1, 'Losing the tract after the incision and passing the tube into the subcutaneous tissues of the neck is the most frequent complication.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'After a successful emergency cricothyrotomy, the patient must be transferred to the OR for conversion to a formal tracheostomy. This conversion should ideally occur within:', '["2 to 4 hours", "24 to 72 hours", "1 to 2 weeks", "Never, it can serve as a permanent airway"]', 1, 'A cricothyrotomy tube irritates the vocal cords and cricoid cartilage. To prevent long-term subglottic stenosis, it must be converted to a tracheostomy within 24-72 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When positioning a 2-year-old child for intubation, placing a small roll under the shoulders is often necessary because of their:', '["Short neck", "Large, prominent occiput", "Anterior larynx", "Large tongue"]', 1, 'A child''s large occiput forces the neck into flexion when lying supine, which obstructs the airway. A shoulder roll creates the optimal sniffing position.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Anatomically, the pediatric larynx is positioned differently than the adult larynx. It is typically:', '["More posterior and inferior", "More anterior and superior", "More posterior and superior", "More anterior and inferior"]', 1, 'The pediatric larynx is more anterior and superior (cephalad), usually at the level of C3-C4 compared to C4-C5 in adults, making it more difficult to visualize during direct laryngoscopy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Historically, what was considered the narrowest portion of the pediatric airway (under age 8)?', '["The vocal cords", "The epiglottis", "The cricoid ring", "The trachea"]', 2, 'While recent MRI studies suggest the vocal cords may actually be the narrowest part, the rigid, non-distensible cricoid ring acts as a functional seal, which is why uncuffed tubes were historically preferred in young children.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Modern pediatric airway guidelines now recommend which type of endotracheal tube for almost all children, to reduce the need for tube exchanges and decrease aspiration risk?', '["Uncuffed tubes", "Micro-cuffed tubes", "Double-lumen tubes", "Reinforced (armored) tubes"]', 1, 'Cuffed tubes are now standard for all ages. They allow for lower leak volumes and more accurate ventilation, provided cuff pressures are closely monitored.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'You are intubating a 1-year-old child. You elect to use a straight (Miller) blade rather than a curved (Macintosh) blade. This choice is primarily driven by the fact that infants have:', '["A larger tongue", "A short, stiff, U-shaped epiglottis that needs to be directly lifted", "A more posterior larynx", "Teeth that are easily damaged"]', 1, 'Infants have a large, floppy, omega-shaped epiglottis. A straight blade physically lifts the epiglottis out of the way to visualize the cords.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-year-old child develops severe laryngospasm after extubation. You have attempted positive pressure ventilation and the Larson maneuver without success. The patient is rapidly desaturating. The most appropriate next step is:', '["Surgical cricothyrotomy", "Administer a small dose of Succinylcholine (0.1-0.2 mg/kg) intravenously", "Administer Albuterol via nebulizer", "Needle thoracostomy"]', 1, 'A "spasmolytic" dose of succinylcholine breaks the laryngospasm rapidly, allowing ventilation to resume.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An intubated pediatric patient suddenly deteriorates. You apply the DOPE mnemonic. In pediatrics, which complication is particularly common due to their short tracheal length?', '["Equipment failure", "Tension pneumothorax", "Mainstem intubation or accidental extubation (Displacement)", "Obstruction from massive bleeding"]', 2, 'The distance from the vocal cords to the carina is very short in infants, making accidental endobronchial intubation or complete extubation highly likely with minimal head movement.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During intubation, you auscultate gurgling over the epigastrium, hear no breath sounds in the axillae, and note an absent waveform on capnography. You have most likely intubated the:', '["Trachea", "Right mainstem bronchus", "Esophagus", "Left mainstem bronchus"]', 2, 'These are the classic signs of esophageal intubation. The tube must be immediately removed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Failure to promptly recognize and correct an esophageal intubation rapidly leads to:', '["Subglottic stenosis", "Severe anoxic brain injury and cardiac arrest", "Tension pneumothorax", "Atelectasis"]', 1, 'Unrecognized esophageal intubation is a catastrophic, never-event that causes profound hypoxia and rapid death.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'You have intubated a patient and note breath sounds are loud on the right and absent on the left. Capnography shows a good waveform. The most appropriate initial step is:', '["Remove the tube completely and re-intubate", "Perform a left-sided needle decompression", "Deflate the cuff, withdraw the tube 2 cm, re-inflate, and re-auscultate", "Order an immediate CT scan of the chest"]', 2, 'This indicates a right mainstem intubation. Withdrawing the tube slightly usually corrects the problem and restores bilateral ventilation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'To prevent Ventilator-Associated Pneumonia (VAP) in an intubated ICU patient, what is the recommended angle for the head of the bed?', '["Flat (0 degrees)", "15 degrees", "30 to 45 degrees", "90 degrees"]', 2, 'Elevating the head of the bed 30-45 degrees significantly reduces the aspiration of pooled oropharyngeal secretions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following interventions is part of the standard "VAP bundle" to prevent ventilator-associated pneumonia?', '["Routine prophylactic intravenous antibiotics", "Chlorhexidine oral care", "Keeping the patient deeply sedated at all times", "Changing the ventilator circuit daily"]', 1, 'Chlorhexidine oral care, elevating the head of the bed, DVT prophylaxis, peptic ulcer prophylaxis, and daily sedation vacations are key components of the VAP bundle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Post-intubation hypotension is a highly morbid complication. Which physiological change during intubation directly decreases venous return to the heart, worsening hypotension?', '["Loss of sympathetic tone from induction agents", "The transition from negative-pressure spontaneous breathing to positive-pressure mechanical ventilation", "Bradycardia from vagal stimulation", "Histamine release from paralytics"]', 1, 'Positive pressure ventilation increases intrathoracic pressure, which squeezes the vena cava and decreases venous return (preload) to the right heart.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A critically ill, hypotensive patient requires intubation. To prevent peri-intubation cardiac arrest, the clinician prepares "push-dose pressors." Which medication and dose is commonly used for this purpose?', '["Epinephrine 1 mg IV push", "Epinephrine 10-20 mcg IV push", "Norepinephrine 1000 mcg IV push", "Atropine 1 mg IV push"]', 1, 'Push-dose epinephrine (10-20 mcg/dose) or phenylephrine (50-200 mcg/dose) are used as bridge therapies to rapidly reverse hypotension during intubation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the setting of severe maxillofacial trauma where direct laryngoscopy is predicted to be impossible due to distorted anatomy and massive bleeding, what airway strategy MUST be prepared as the primary backup plan?', '["Blind nasotracheal intubation", "Laryngeal Mask Airway (LMA)", "Surgical cricothyrotomy setup (scalpel-finger-bougie)", "Video laryngoscopy"]', 2, 'Maxillofacial trauma is a classic scenario for a "cannot intubate, cannot ventilate" emergency. A surgical airway must be ready before pushing any induction drugs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with full-thickness burns to the face and neck arrives in the ED. They are currently talking and saturating 100% on room air. The correct airway management strategy is:', '["Observe closely until stridor develops", "Immediate elective intubation before airway edema makes intubation impossible", "Administer high-dose steroids to prevent swelling", "Apply CPAP to splint the airway open"]', 1, 'Airway edema in burn patients progresses rapidly and relentlessly. Prophylactic intubation while the airway is still manageable is critical; waiting until obstruction occurs often results in a fatal CICV situation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When assessing a patient for extubation, which of the following criteria suggests they can adequately protect their airway?', '["Inability to lift their head off the bed", "No gag reflex", "Following simple commands and sustained head lift for 5 seconds", "A respiratory rate of 35 breaths per minute"]', 2, 'A sustained head lift for 5 seconds indicates adequate recovery of neuromuscular function and the ability to clear secretions and protect the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Two hours after extubation, a patient develops inspiratory stridor. This is most likely due to:', '["Laryngospasm", "Laryngeal edema", "Pulmonary embolism", "Pneumothorax"]', 1, 'Post-extubation stridor is caused by swelling (edema) of the vocal cords or glottic structures from the trauma of the endotracheal tube.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which pharmacological treatment is indicated for a patient presenting with post-extubation stridor?', '["Intravenous Amiodarone", "Nebulized Racemic Epinephrine and IV Dexamethasone", "Intravenous Furosemide", "Nebulized Albuterol"]', 1, 'Racemic epinephrine causes vasoconstriction, rapidly reducing airway mucosal edema, while steroids provide longer-term anti-inflammatory effects.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A ventilator alarms for "High Peak Airway Pressure." You check the plateau pressure and find it is normal. This indicates a problem with:', '["Lung compliance (e.g., ARDS, pulmonary edema)", "Airway resistance (e.g., kinked tube, bronchospasm, biting the tube)", "Hypovolemia", "Neuromuscular weakness"]', 1, 'A high peak pressure with a normal plateau pressure indicates a resistance problem in the airways or the circuit (e.g., tube kink, asthma).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A ventilator alarms for "High Peak Airway Pressure." You check the plateau pressure and find it is ALSO highly elevated. This indicates a problem with:', '["Airway resistance", "Patient biting the endotracheal tube", "Lung compliance (e.g., Tension pneumothorax, ARDS, pulmonary edema)", "A disconnected ventilator circuit"]', 2, 'Elevated plateau pressures indicate stiff, non-compliant lungs or external compression (e.g., tension pneumothorax, pulmonary edema, ARDS).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the event of an unplanned, accidental extubation of an ICU patient, the very first action a nurse or physician must take is:', '["Call for a portable chest X-ray", "Begin positive pressure ventilation with a bag-valve-mask (BVM)", "Administer a full dose of Propofol", "Push push-dose epinephrine"]', 1, 'The immediate priority is to support oxygenation and ventilation manually with a BVM while preparing for re-intubation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A "cuff leak" around an endotracheal tube is most likely to result in which of the following mechanical ventilation issues?', '["Inadequate delivered tidal volume and loss of PEEP", "Excessive PEEP", "High peak airway pressures", "Tension pneumothorax"]', 0, 'A leaking cuff allows air to escape around the tube, meaning the ventilator cannot deliver the full tidal volume or maintain Positive End-Expiratory Pressure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which complication is characterized by a hoarse voice and difficulty swallowing weeks after a prolonged, traumatic intubation?', '["Subglottic stenosis", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Tracheoesophageal fistula", "Pneumomediastinum"]', 1, 'Trauma to the recurrent laryngeal nerve or direct trauma to the arytenoid cartilages during intubation can lead to vocal cord paralysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Retrograde intubation is a rarely used technique that involves:', '["Passing the tube blindly through the nose", "Passing a guidewire through the cricothyroid membrane, up through the vocal cords, out the mouth, and feeding the tube over it", "Intubating the esophagus intentionally", "Using a bronchoscope to intubate from the trachea upwards"]', 1, 'This technique is a salvage maneuver used when the glottis cannot be visualized from above, but the airway is not completely obstructed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Digital (tactile) intubation involves using the index and middle fingers to palpate the epiglottis and guide the endotracheal tube into the glottis. It is primarily indicated when:', '["The patient has an anticipated difficult airway in the OR", "The patient is deeply comatose, has copious blood/secretions obscuring vision, and is trapped (e.g., pinned in a car)", "The patient is awake and agitated", "The patient is a neonate"]', 1, 'Digital intubation is a blind technique useful when heavy bleeding or physical entrapment makes standard laryngoscopy impossible. The patient MUST be deeply unconscious/paralyzed to prevent biting the provider''s fingers.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The Intubating Laryngeal Mask Airway (ILMA, e.g., Fastrach) is uniquely designed to allow for:', '["Administration of nebulized medications", "Blind passage of an endotracheal tube directly through the LMA into the trachea", "Surgical access to the cricothyroid membrane", "Continuous suctioning of the esophagus"]', 1, 'The ILMA has a rigid handle and a channel designed to direct a specialized endotracheal tube blindly through the vocal cords.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary disadvantage or limitation of Video Laryngoscopy (VL) compared to Direct Laryngoscopy (DL)?', '["VL requires greater cervical spine extension", "VL provides a narrower field of view", "The camera lens on the VL blade can easily be obscured by blood, vomit, or secretions", "VL cannot be used with a bougie"]', 2, 'In massive airway soiling (e.g., massive GI bleed, severe trauma), blood or vomitus quickly covers the camera lens, rendering the device useless.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The Sellick maneuver involves applying posterior pressure to the cricoid cartilage. Historically, its intended purpose was to:', '["Improve the view of the glottis", "Compress the esophagus against the cervical vertebrae to prevent passive regurgitation and aspiration", "Stimulate a vagal response to lower heart rate", "Prevent air from entering the stomach during BVM ventilation"]', 1, 'While widely taught historically, modern evidence shows it frequently distorts airway anatomy, worsens the glottic view, and may not reliably prevent aspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Magill forceps are specially angled forceps utilized during airway management primarily to:', '["Clamp the endotracheal tube to prevent leaks", "Remove foreign bodies from the pharynx or guide a nasotracheal tube into the glottis", "Retract the tongue", "Puncture the cricothyroid membrane"]', 1, 'Magill forceps are used under direct visualization to grab foreign objects or to manually direct the tip of a nasotracheal tube into the vocal cords.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old unimmunized child presents with high fever, drooling, a muffled "hot potato" voice, and is sitting in the tripod position. Stridor is present. What is the most appropriate management regarding their airway?', '["Immediate awake fiberoptic intubation in the ED", "Immediate blind nasotracheal intubation", "Keep the child calm, provide blow-by oxygen, and mobilize anesthesia/ENT for intubation in the operating room", "Perform an immediate prophylactic cricothyrotomy"]', 2, 'This is classic acute epiglottitis. Any agitation (e.g., IV placement, laying flat) can precipitate complete airway obstruction. They should be intubated under deep inhalational anesthesia in the OR by experts.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis) typically presents with a "barking" cough and inspiratory stridor. First-line management to decrease airway edema includes:', '["Intravenous antibiotics", "Dexamethasone and nebulized Racemic Epinephrine", "Immediate intubation", "Tracheostomy"]', 1, 'Croup is a viral infection. Steroids reduce inflammation over hours, while racemic epinephrine provides rapid, temporary relief of mucosal edema. Intubation is rarely required.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Ludwig''s angina is a rapidly progressive cellulitis of the submandibular space. Why is airway management in these patients considered extremely high-risk?', '["The infection destroys the vocal cords", "Massive swelling of the tongue and floor of the mouth obliterates the airway anatomy, making direct laryngoscopy impossible", "The bacteria produce a toxin that causes laryngospasm", "The infection makes the patient immune to paralytics"]', 1, 'The upward displacement of the tongue creates a severe "cannot intubate, cannot ventilate" risk. Awake fiberoptic intubation or an awake tracheostomy are the preferred methods.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During intubation, you are attempting to secure the airway of a patient with severe facial burns. Why must Succinylcholine be avoided if the injury occurred 3 days ago?', '["It causes fatal bradycardia in burn patients", "It triggers massive potassium release from upregulated extrajunctional acetylcholine receptors, leading to lethal hyperkalemia", "It is ineffective in burn patients", "It causes malignant hyperthermia specifically in burn victims"]', 1, 'Major burns, crush injuries, and upper motor neuron lesions lead to widespread proliferation of acetylcholine receptors. Depolarization by succinylcholine causes massive potassium efflux.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on a mechanical ventilator has a sudden drop in oxygen saturation, tachycardia, hypotension, and absent breath sounds on the right. Tracheal deviation to the left is noted. The immediate life-saving intervention is:', '["Needle decompression of the right chest", "Increasing the PEEP on the ventilator", "Administering a fluid bolus", "Extubating the patient"]', 0, 'These are classic signs of a tension pneumothorax, a known complication of positive pressure ventilation (barotrauma). Immediate decompression is required.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes the correct measurement of an oropharyngeal airway (OPA) prior to insertion?', '["From the tip of the nose to the earlobe", "From the corner of the mouth to the angle of the mandible", "From the center of the mouth to the bottom of the neck", "From the tip of the nose to the tragus"]', 1, 'Measuring from the corner of the mouth to the angle of the jaw ensures the OPA will properly hold the tongue forward without pushing it back into the glottis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A nasopharyngeal airway (NPA) is contraindicated in which of the following clinical scenarios?', '["A patient with an intact gag reflex", "A patient with a suspected basilar skull fracture or severe midface trauma", "A semi-conscious patient requiring BVM ventilation", "A patient with asthma"]', 1, 'An NPA can inadvertently be passed through a fractured cribriform plate into the cranial vault.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which statement correctly compares a Mac (curved) blade to a Miller (straight) blade?', '["The Miller blade rests in the vallecula, while the Mac blade lifts the epiglottis directly", "The Mac blade rests in the vallecula, while the Miller blade lifts the epiglottis directly", "The Mac blade is preferred for pediatric patients", "The Miller blade requires less force to use"]', 1, 'The Macintosh blade indirectly lifts the epiglottis by applying tension to the hyoepiglottic ligament in the vallecula, while the Miller blade goes past the epiglottis to lift it directly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The use of a Gum Elastic Bougie is most strongly indicated when encountering which Cormack-Lehane laryngoscopy view?', '["Grade 1 (Full glottis)", "Grade 2 (Partial glottis)", "Grade 3 (Epiglottis only visible)", "Grade 4 (Soft palate only visible)"]', 2, 'With a Grade 3 view, the bougie can be passed blindly behind the epiglottis, feeling for tracheal clicks to confirm placement before the tube is railroaded over it. It is ineffective in a Grade 4 view because there are no landmarks to guide it.');
